    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d320">

                <pb facs="0550" n="418" xml:id="pg418"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">711.60d/7–1545</note>
                <head>No. 320<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">The Second Secretary of Embassy in the
                        United Kingdom</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Thompson</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Assistant Secretary of State</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Dunn</hi>)<anchor n="1" xml:id="d320-f1"/>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">secret</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Babelsberg</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1945-07-15">July 15, 1945</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p rendition="#center">
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Memorandum</hi>
                </p>
                <p rendition="#flush">Subject: Finland</p>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Mr. Dunn</hi>: … Stalin informed Churchill on June
                        14<anchor n="2" xml:id="d320-f2"/> that he saw no reason to defer the
                    restoration of diplomatic relations with Finland, which was fulfilling the
                    armistice conditions.<anchor n="3" xml:id="d320-f3"/> It will be recalled that
                    the Soviets had earlier informed us that they did not consider that the
                    resumption of diplomatic relations with Finland before the resumption of
                    relations with Rumania and Bulgaria would be justified.<anchor n="4"
                        xml:id="d320-f4"/>
                </p>
                <p>Since we have never been at war with Finland<anchor n="5" xml:id="d320-f5"/> we
                    are in a better position to resume relations than the British and Soviets and it
                    is believed that we should do so as soon as this can be accomplished without
                    seriously offending the Russians. At this meeting it would appear advisable to
                    take any opportunity that may present itself to clear the way for this
                    action.</p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d320-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d320-f1-content">
                <p>Printed from the ribbon copy, which is unsigned.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d320-f2" n="2" xml:id="d320-f2-content">
                <p>See <hi rendition="#italic"> Stalin’s Correspondence</hi>, <ref
                        target="frus1945Berlinv01#pg_363">vol. <hi rendition="#small-caps">i</hi>,
                        p. 363</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d320-f3" n="3" xml:id="d320-f3-content">
                <p>i. e., the terms of the armistice (to which the United States was not a party)
                    signed at Moscow, September 19, 1944. Text in <hi rendition="#italic">British
                        and Foreign State Papers</hi>, vol. <hi rendition="#small-caps">cxlv</hi>,
                    p. 513.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d320-f4" n="4" xml:id="d320-f4-content">
                <p>See <ref target="#d285-f5">document No. 285, footnote 5</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d320-f5" n="5" xml:id="d320-f5-content">
                <p>The United States severed diplomatic relations with Finland on June 30, 1944, but
                    at no time during World War II did it declare war on Finland.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
